  "body": "`TestRateLimitIsWaitedOut` (internal/gateway/retry_test.go) failed once in a full `go test -tags nogui -timeout 30m ./internal/gateway/` run on a heavily loaded Mac (load average ~190, several sessions' suites running together):\n\n```\n--- FAIL: TestRateLimitIsWaitedOut (5.56s)\n    retry_test.go:243: an hour away: passed on after 1.875588417s\nFAIL\tgithub.com/yetone/magpie/internal/gateway\t1093.661s\n```\n\nThe tree was origin/main 3a9d73c5c plus an unrelated change: the Image recognition / searcher reasoning level, which touches vision.go, search.go and search_kimi.go and nothing on the retry path.\n\nOn freshly fetched origin/main 41972b25f the test passes:\n- alone, `-count=10`;\n- in 8 parallel copies of the test binary, each `-test.cpu 1 -test.count=5`.\n\nThe \"an hour away\" case (429 with `Retry-After: 3600`) checks that the 429 is the agent's at once, using a 1s wall-clock bound (`x.n == 1 && time.Since(start) > time.Second`). The vendor was asked only once (the try count passed), so the time went to something else on that path: the server's setup, the first request's routing, and the like. A busy machine's scheduler can take that long.\n\nThis is a timing assumption on one side. Per the code standards, either:\n- measure only the part that could wait (between the 429 and the reply), or\n- inject the delay to tell a real wait apart from a slow machine.\n\nNot fixed here.\n",
  "closed_by": null,
